## 2.5 SAP Profile Files — Deep Dive

### Profile Hierarchy:
```
Level 1: DEFAULT.PFL
         ↓ (all instances read this first)
Level 2: START Profile (START_<instance>_<host>)
         ↓ (defines which processes to start)
Level 3: Instance Profile (<SID>_<instance>_<host>)
           (instance-specific runtime parameters — overrides DEFAULT)
```

### Common Profile Parameters:
```
Parameter                           Example Value   Description
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
login/system_client               = 100            Default login client
rdisp/max_wprun_time              = 300            Max dialog WP time (sec)
rdisp/wp_no_dia                   = 10             # of dialog WPs
rdisp/wp_no_btc                   = 5              # of background WPs
icm/server_port_0                 = PROT=HTTP,PORT=8000
login/fails_to_user_lock          = 5              Lockout after 5 fails
login/password_expiration_time    = 90             Password expiry (days)
abap/buffersize                   = 500000         ABAP program buffer
zcsa/table_buffer_area            = 30000          Table buffer size
em/initial_size_MB                = 3000           Extended memory size
rdisp/msserv                      = sapms<SID>     Message server service
```

### Managing Profiles (RZ10):
```
Transaction: RZ10 → Profile Maintenance

Steps:
1. Select profile name from dropdown
2. Choose Edit Mode:
   ► Basic Maintenance  → Simple key=value edit
   ► Extended Maintenance → Parameter-by-parameter (recommended!)
3. Modify values
4. ALWAYS click "Copy" then "Activate profile"
   (Activation saves to database — critical!)
5. Restart instance for changes to take effect

⚠️ GOLDEN RULE: NEVER edit profile files directly with vi/notepad!
   Always use RZ10 → keeps filesystem and DB in sync
   
Transaction: RZ11 → Documentation for any individual parameter
```

