## 4.1 SAP System Start/Stop Sequence

### CORRECT START SEQUENCE:
```
STEP 1: Start Database
        ────────────────────────────────────────────
        Oracle:  su - ora<sid> → sqlplus / as sysdba → startup
        HANA:    HDB start (as <sid>adm)
        MSSQL:   Start SQL Server service (Windows Services)

STEP 2: Start SAP Central Services (ASCS/SCS)
        ────────────────────────────────────────────
        Contains: Message Server + Enqueue Server
        Must start BEFORE application servers!

STEP 3: Start SAP Application Server(s)
        ────────────────────────────────────────────
        Primary Application Server (PAS / CI)
        Then Additional Application Servers (AAS/DI)
```

### CORRECT STOP SEQUENCE (REVERSE order!):
```
STEP 1: Stop Additional Application Servers (AAS/DI)
STEP 2: Stop Primary Application Server (PAS/CI)
STEP 3: Stop Central Services (ASCS/SCS)
STEP 4: Stop Database

⚠️ NEVER stop the database before SAP! 
   This causes data corruption and update failures.
```

---

## 4.2 Starting SAP — All Methods

### Method 1: Using `startsap` Command (Unix/Linux)
```bash
# Switch to SAP admin user
su - <sid>adm      # e.g., su - devadm

# Start specific instance
startsap r3         # Start ABAP stack
startsap j2ee       # Start Java stack
startsap all        # Start everything

# Example for SID=DEV, Instance=00
startsap r3 DEV 00
```

### Method 2: Using `sapcontrol` Command
```bash
# Start the SAP system
sapcontrol -nr <NN> -function StartSystem ALL

# Start a specific service
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function StartService DEV

# Check current status
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function GetProcessList

# Check start/stop history
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function GetStartProfile
```

### Method 3: SAP Management Console (Windows MMC)
```
Windows Start → SAP Management Console
  ├── Right-click SAP Instance
  ├── Select "Start" or "Stop"
  └── Monitor status in the console window
```

### Method 4: SAP GUI Transaction (Basis Admin)
```
Transaction: SM50 → Verify work processes came up
Transaction: SM51 → List of all application servers (verify all up)
Transaction: SM21 → Check system log for startup errors
```

---

## 4.3 Stopping SAP — All Methods

```bash
# Graceful stop (recommended — waits for processes to finish)
stopsap r3           # Stop ABAP instance
stopsap all          # Stop all instances

# Using sapcontrol
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function StopSystem ALL
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function Stop

# Force stop (use with caution!)
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function StopSystem ALL -timeout 60

# Kill all SAP processes (last resort - OS level)
# ⚠️ DO NOT use unless system is completely hung!
kill -9 $(ps -ef | grep <SID> | awk '{print $2}')
```

## 4.4 Checking SAP Process Status

```bash
# Check running SAP processes
ps -ef | grep <SID>

# Check specific SAP services
sapcontrol -nr 00 -function GetProcessList

# Output explanation:
#  disp+work → Dispatcher (MUST be running)
#  icman     → ICM (for web access)
#  gwrd      → Gateway
#  msg_server → Message Server (on CI only)
#  enserver  → Enqueue Server (on CI only)
```

## 4.5 Startup Troubleshooting

```
SAP won't start? Follow this checklist:

1. CHECK DATABASE FIRST:
   Is the database running? If not → SAP cannot connect!
   
2. CHECK DISK SPACE:
   df -h → Ensure /usr/sap and /sapmnt have > 10% free

3. CHECK DISPATCHER TRACE:
   cat /usr/sap/<SID>/<Instance>/work/dev_disp
   Common errors:
   ► "db_connect failed" → DB not started or wrong params
   ► "no more shared memory" → OS memory issue
   ► "port already in use" → Another process on same port
   
4. CHECK MESSAGE SERVER:
   cat /usr/sap/<SID>/<Instance>/work/dev_ms
   
5. CHECK PROFILE SYNTAX:
   RZ10 → Verify no syntax errors in profiles
   
6. CHECK SAP SYSTEM LOG:
   SM21 → Filter for errors around startup time
```

