## 11.1 Understanding SAP Landscape Concepts

```
The SAP System Landscape defines HOW systems are organized
and HOW changes flow between them.

CORE PRINCIPLE:
  ► Never directly change PRD
  ► All changes go: DEV → QAS → PRD
  ► Changes are controlled via Transport Requests
  ► Testing happens in QAS before going to PRD
```

## 11.2 Standard Three-System Landscape

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    STANDARD 3-SYSTEM LANDSCAPE                    │
│                                                                    │
│  ┌─────────────────┐                                             │
│  │   DEV (SID:DEV) │                                             │
│  │─────────────────│                                             │
│  │ Role: Development│                                             │
│  │ Client: 100     │──Transport──▶  ┌─────────────────┐         │
│  │ Users: Devs     │                │   QAS (SID:QAS) │         │
│  │ CATT: Allowed   │                │─────────────────│         │
│  │ Changes: Open   │                │ Role: Quality   │         │
│  └─────────────────┘                │ Client: 200     │──Trsp──▶│
│                                     │ Users: Testers  │         │
│                                     │ Changes: Locked │         │
│                                     └─────────────────┘         │
│                                                          ▼        │
│                                             ┌─────────────────┐  │
│                                             │   PRD (SID:PRD) │  │
│                                             │─────────────────│  │
│                                             │ Role: Production │  │
│                                             │ Client: 300     │  │
│                                             │ Users: Business │  │
│                                             │ Changes: LOCKED │  │
│                                             └─────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## 11.3 Two-System Landscape

```
Used when: Budget constraints or simpler implementations

DEV ──Transport──▶ PRD

Risks:
  ❌ Untested code goes directly to production
  ❌ No separate QAS for user acceptance testing
  ❌ Rollback is harder
  ✅ Lower infrastructure costs
  ✅ Simpler to manage

⚠️ NOT recommended for large or complex SAP systems!
```

## 11.4 Five-System Landscape (Enterprise)

```
Used by: Large enterprises with complex change management

  DEV → INT (Integration Test) → QAS (UAT) → PRE (Pre-Prod) → PRD

Purpose of Each:
  DEV  → Development and unit testing
  INT  → Integration testing (all modules together)
  QAS  → User acceptance testing (business users)
  PRE  → Pre-production / Performance test (mirror of PRD)
  PRD  → Live production

Benefits:
  ✅ Thorough testing before production
  ✅ Performance testing in PRE (same spec as PRD)
  ✅ Business sign-off before go-live
  ❌ Higher infrastructure cost
  ❌ More complex transport routes
```

## 11.5 Landscape Planning Considerations

```
KEY DECISIONS when planning an SAP landscape:

1. NUMBER OF SYSTEMS:
   ► How many systems needed?
   ► Budget vs. risk tolerance

2. HARDWARE SIZING:
   ► PRD must be sized for PEAK load + 30% headroom
   ► QAS should mirror PRD sizing as closely as possible
   ► DEV can be smaller

3. CLIENT STRATEGY:
   ► How many clients per system?
   ► One client per company or multiple?
   ► Separate clients for training?

4. TRANSPORT ROUTES:
   ► Linear (DEV→QAS→PRD) — standard
   ► Parallel (multiple DEV systems feeding same PRD)

5. NAMING CONVENTIONS:
   ► SID naming strategy
   ► Client numbering standards
   ► Transport request naming

6. SHARED vs. DEDICATED:
   ► Shared transport directory (NFS mount)
   ► OS platform consistency across landscape
```

