## 15.1 SAP Client — Complete Concept

```
A CLIENT is an organizationally independent unit within an SAP system.

What is CLIENT-SPECIFIC (per client):
  ✅ User master records (who can log in)
  ✅ Business data (FI postings, purchase orders, etc.)
  ✅ Customizing/configuration settings
  ✅ Roles and authorization data
  ✅ Background job definitions

What is CROSS-CLIENT (shared across all clients):
  ❌ ABAP programs and repository objects
  ❌ Data Dictionary (table definitions, not data)
  ❌ Kernel / executable programs
  ❌ Transport configuration (STMS)
  ❌ System profiles

PRACTICAL IMPLICATION:
  If developer creates ABAP program ZTEST in Client 100 of DEV:
  → The program exists in ALL clients of that DEV system!
  → It's visible and runnable in Client 200 of same DEV too!
```

## 15.2 Client Settings (SCC4) — Deep Dive

```
Transaction: SCC4 → Client Maintenance

IMPORTANT SETTINGS EXPLAINED:

1. CLIENT ROLE:
╔═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ Role                  ║ Typical Use       ║ Restriction     ║
╠═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╣
║ Production (P)        ║ Live PRD client   ║ Highest locks   ║
║ Test (T)              ║ QAS testing       ║ Medium locks    ║
║ Customizing (C)       ║ DEV config        ║ Open for config ║
║ Demo (D)              ║ Demos             ║ Flexible        ║
║ Training/Education (E)║ User training     ║ Flexible        ║
║ SAP Reference (S)     ║ Client 000 only   ║ No modification ║
╚═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

2. CHANGES AND TRANSPORTS (for client-specific objects):
  ► "No changes allowed"
    → Completely locked. No config changes possible.
    → Use for PRD client after go-live.
    
  ► "Changes without automatic recording"
    → Changes possible but NOT captured in transports.
    → Use carefully! Changes lost if client refreshed.
    
  ► "Automatic recording of changes" ⭐ (DEV standard)
    → Every change automatically goes into a transport.
    → Best practice for DEV client.
    
  ► "Changes without recording, no transports allowed"
    → One-time manual changes allowed (emergency use).

3. CROSS-CLIENT OBJECT CHANGES:
  ► "Changes to repository and cross-client Customizing allowed"
    → Allows ABAP development and cross-client changes.
    → Only in DEV!
    
  ► "No changes to cross-client objects" ⭐ (QAS/PRD standard)
    → Prevents modification of ABAP programs, DD objects.
    → MUST be set in QAS and PRD!

4. PROTECTION LEVEL:
  ► Level 0: No protection (client can be source for copies)
  ► Level 1: No overwriting via client copy or transport
  ► Level 2: No overwriting, no deletion (maximum protection)
  → PRD should be Level 2!
```

## 15.3 Local Client Copy (SCCL) — Step-by-Step

```
Transaction: SCCL → Local Client Copy

SCENARIO: Create new client 110 from client 000 (same DEV system)

PRE-STEPS:
1. Create the target client first:
   SCC4 → New Entries → Enter client 110, description, role
   Save ✓

2. Log ON to the TARGET client (110):
   SAP GUI → Logon Client = 110 → User = SAP* → Pwd = PASS (default)
   
   ⚠️ SAP* with password PASS is the only user in a new client!
      Immediately change or lock SAP* after copy!

3. Run SCCL:

COPY PROFILE OPTIONS:
╔══════════════════╦══════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ Profile          ║ What gets copied                                 ║
╠══════════════════╬══════════════════════════════════════════════════╣
║ SAP_ALL          ║ Everything: customizing + user data + app data  ║
║ SAP_CUSTOMIZING  ║ Only customizing settings (no business data)    ║
║ SAP_UCUS         ║ User masters + customizing (no business data)   ║
║ SAP_UZER         ║ User masters only                               ║
║ SAP_CUST_NOREM   ║ Customizing without Remittance                  ║
╚══════════════════╩══════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

FIELDS:
  Profile        : SAP_CUSTOMIZING
  Source Client  : 000
  Target Client  : 110 (auto-filled — current logon client)
  Copy Users     : ✅ (include user master records)

EXECUTION:
  □ Test Run first → Check for errors
  ✅ Schedule as Background Job → Always!
  
  Click Execute → Choose "Background" → Schedule immediately

MONITORING:
  SCC3 → Client Copy Log
  Shows: Steps completed, objects copied, errors (if any)
  Refresh periodically — large copies take HOURS

POST-COPY STEPS:
  1. SCC4 → Set appropriate restrictions on new client
  2. Change SAP* password in new client
  3. Create Basis admin user in new client
  4. Verify data looks correct (spot check)
```

## 15.4 Remote Client Copy (SCC9)

```
Transaction: SCC9 → Remote Client Copy

SCENARIO: Copy PRD client 300 to QAS client 200 (system refresh)

PRE-REQUISITES:
  1. RFC connection from QAS to PRD exists (SM59 on QAS)
  2. Copy user in PRD has authorization: S_CLNT_IMP
  3. QAS has enough disk/DB space for the copy
  4. Inform QAS users → system will be unavailable during copy

STEPS:
  1. Log on to QAS, Client 200 (target)
  2. Run SCC9:
     Profile          : SAP_ALL
     Source System    : PRD_RFC          (RFC destination name)
     Source Client    : 300
     Target Client    : 200 (current)
     Copy Users       : ✅
  
  3. Test Run first → Verify RFC works and space available
  4. Execute as Background Job
  5. Monitor: SCC3 on QAS

POST-COPY ACTIONS (MANDATORY!):
  ✅ Reset all user passwords in QAS
  ✅ Lock all productive users (prevent confusion with PRD!)
  ✅ Change system parameters for QAS (batch servers, printers)
  ✅ Update RFC destinations to point to QAS systems
  ✅ Run BDLS (Logical System Name conversion)
  ✅ Inform team that QAS is refreshed
```

## 15.5 Client Export and Import (SCC8/SCC7)

```
MOST CONTROLLED METHOD — Used when:
  ► Source and target are NOT directly connected via RFC
  ► Large client copies across different networks
  ► Documented audit trail required

EXPORT PHASE (on Source System):
Transaction: SCC8 → Client Export

  1. Run on SOURCE system (e.g., PRD)
  2. Select Profile: SAP_ALL
  3. Target System: QAS  
  4. Execute → Creates transport requests
  5. RELEASE the transport requests (SE01 → Release)
  6. Transport files appear in /usr/sap/trans/

TRANSPORT PHASE:
  Copy transport files from PRD transport dir to QAS transport dir
  (If on same NFS share → automatic)
  (If different servers → use FTP/SCP: scp R<nr>.PRD user@qas:/usr/sap/trans/data/)

IMPORT PHASE (on Target System):
Transaction: STMS → Import Queue for QAS
  Import the export transport requests

POST-IMPORT PHASE:
Transaction: SCC7 → Post-Import Actions
  Run on TARGET system to complete the client copy
  ► Adjusts internal references
  ► Cleans up temporary data
  ► MANDATORY — don't skip this step!

VERIFY:
Transaction: SCC3 → Check copy was successful
```

## 15.6 Client Deletion (SCC5)

```
Transaction: SCC5 → Delete Client

⛔ THIS IS COMPLETELY IRREVERSIBLE! NO UNDO! ⛔

WHEN TO DELETE A CLIENT:
  ► Decommissioning old training client
  ► Removing obsolete test client
  ► Cleaning up after project

STEPS:
  1. SCC5 → Enter client number
  2. ✅ Test Run first — see what will be deleted
  3. Get written approval from management
  4. Inform all users
  5. Execute deletion (runs in background)
  6. Monitor: SCC3 → Client Delete log

SAFER ALTERNATIVE — Locking the client:
  SCC4 → Set client status to:
  "No changes allowed" + Protection Level 2
  → Client exists but cannot be used
  → Soft deactivation — recoverable!
```

