## 22.1 What is Workload Analysis?

```
Workload Analysis = Understanding HOW your SAP system
                    is performing and WHERE bottlenecks are.

WHY IT MATTERS:
  ► Business complains "SAP is slow today"
  → Workload analysis tells you: WHERE exactly is it slow?
     Is it: DB? Network? ABAP? Memory? Work processes?

WORKLOAD ANALYSIS TOOLS:
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ T-Code │ Tool Name                │ What It Shows            │
├────────┼──────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────┤
│ SM66   │ Global WP Monitor        │ Current WP activity      │
│ ST05   │ SQL Trace                │ SQL statements to DB     │
│ ST12   │ ABAP+Performance Trace   │ ABAP + SQL combined      │
│ SM50   │ Local WP Monitor         │ Local instance WPs       │
│ ST07   │ Application Monitor      │ Users by transaction     │
│ ST02   │ Buffer Monitor           │ Buffer hit rates         │
│ SM04   │ User Overview            │ Active user sessions     │
│ AL05   │ Monitor Current Workload │ Real-time workload       │
│ STAD   │ Statistical Records      │ Historical response times│
│ SM12   │ Lock Entries             │ Locked objects (blocks)  │
└────────┴──────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────┘
```

## 22.2 Workload Monitor — STAD

```
Transaction: STAD → Statistical Records Analysis
(Also called: Transaction Workload Monitor)

PURPOSE:
  STAD shows HISTORICAL performance data for all transactions.
  Every SAP dialog step creates a statistical record.
  Records retained for: ~24-48 hours (configurable)

HOW TO USE STAD:
  1. STAD → Select time range (e.g., last 2 hours)
  2. Optional filters:
     - User name
     - Transaction code
     - Client
     - Server (which app server)
  3. Execute → List of all transactions with response times

KEY METRICS IN STAD:
╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ Metric          ║ Description              ║ Good Value       ║
╠═════════════════╬══════════════════════════╬══════════════════╣
║ Response Time   ║ Total time for step      ║ < 1 second       ║
║ CPU Time        ║ Time spent on CPU        ║ < 300ms          ║
║ DB Request Time ║ Time waiting for DB      ║ < 40% of Resp.   ║
║ DB Requests     ║ Number of DB calls       ║ < 10,000         ║
║ Wait for WP     ║ Queue time for free WP   ║ < 50ms           ║
║ Load+Gen Time   ║ ABAP load/generation     ║ < 100ms          ║
║ Roll Time       ║ Roll in/out time         ║ < 50ms           ║
╚═════════════════╩══════════════════════════╩══════════════════╝

RESPONSE TIME BREAKDOWN:
  Total Response Time = 
    Wait for WP          (queue time)
  + Load + Generate Time (ABAP load)
  + DB Request Time      (database)
  + CPU Time             (processing)
  + RFC + CPIC Time      (external calls)
  + Roll Time            (context switch)
  + Other               (remaining)

EXAMPLE ANALYSIS:
  Transaction FB01 showing 8 second response time:
  
  Wait for WP    : 6,000 ms ← ⚠️ BOTTLENECK!
  DB Request Time: 500 ms   ← Normal
  CPU Time       : 200 ms   ← Normal
  
  DIAGNOSIS: Dialog work processes are fully occupied
  ACTION: Add more dialog WPs or optimize running programs
```

## 22.3 SQL Trace Analysis (ST05)

```
Transaction: ST05 → SQL Trace (Performance Trace)

PURPOSE:
  Captures EVERY SQL statement sent from SAP to the database.
  Used to identify slow/expensive database queries.

HOW TO RUN:
  Step 1: ST05 → "Activate Trace"
            ► For specific user: Enter username
            ► For current user: Leave blank
            ► Trace types:
               ✅ SQL Trace  (database calls)
               ✅ RFC Trace  (RFC calls)
               □  HTTP Trace (web requests)

  Step 2: Go to SAP → Reproduce the slow transaction
            ► Perform exactly the slow action

  Step 3: ST05 → "Deactivate Trace"
