## 24.5 SAP Security Updates 2022 — Critical Changes

```
SAP released several CRITICAL security updates in 2022.
Every Basis admin must know these!

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1. GATEWAY SECURITY (secinfo / reginfo) — MANDATORY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
Background: SAP gateway allows external programs to register.
            Without controls = security vulnerability!

secinfo file: Controls which programs can START via gateway
reginfo file: Controls which programs can REGISTER as RFC server

Location:
  /usr/sap/<SID>/<Instance>/data/gw/secinfo
  /usr/sap/<SID>/<Instance>/data/gw/reginfo

Profile Parameters:
  gw/sec_info  = $(DIR_DATA)/gw/secinfo   ← Point to file
  gw/reg_info  = $(DIR_DATA)/gw/reginfo   ← Point to file
  gw/reg_no_conn_info = 1                  ← Enforce reginfo

Minimum secure secinfo content:
  VERSION=2
  #
  # Allow local SAP programs
  P TP=* HOST=local, local ACCESS=*
  #
  # Deny everything else
  D TP=* HOST=* ACCESS=*

Monitoring Gateway:
  SMGW → Logged On Clients (see active RFC connections)
  SMGW → Goto → Expert Functions → Display Secinfo/Reginfo

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2. RFC SECURITY — TRUSTED SYSTEM CONTROLS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SAP Note 3089413 — Tightened trusted RFC settings

New Behavior (kernel 7.53+):
  ► Trusted RFC calls now check the CALLING USER exists
    in the target system and has proper authorizations
  ► Old behavior: Trust relationship = skip user check
  ► New behavior: User must exist AND have S_RFCACL auth

Action Required:
  1. SMTRUST → Review all trusted system configurations
  2. Ensure calling users exist in target systems
  3. Assign S_RFCACL authorization correctly:
     Object: S_RFCACL
     RFCTRUSTL = (trust level of the calling system)
     RFCSYSID  = (SID of the calling system)
     RFCSNC    = (SNC settings)

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
3. HTTP SECURITY — TLS ENFORCEMENT
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SAP strongly recommends disabling HTTP (unencrypted)
and enforcing HTTPS for all communications.

Key Parameters:
  icm/server_port_0 = PROT=HTTPS,PORT=44300    ← HTTPS port
  icm/server_port_1 = PROT=HTTP,PORT=8000,TIMEOUT=3600
  
  # To BLOCK HTTP and force redirect to HTTPS:
  icm/HTTP/redirect_0 = PREFIX=/,TO=https://$(HTTP_HOST):44300$(REQUEST_URI)

TLS Version Control:
  ssl/ciphersuites   = 135:PFS:HIGH::EC_P256:EC_HIGH
  # Disable weak TLS versions:
  ssl/client_cipherlistv2 = 135:PFS:HIGH::EC_P256:EC_HIGH

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
4. LOG4J VULNERABILITY RESPONSE (CVE-2021-44228)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
"Log4Shell" affected SAP Java-based products in 2022.

Affected SAP Products:
  ► SAP NetWeaver Application Server Java
  ► SAP BusinessObjects
  ► SAP Data Intelligence

Response Actions:
  1. Check SAP Security Note 3129883 (Log4j guidance)
  2. Apply emergency SAP patches via JSPM
  3. For immediate mitigation: set JVM parameter:
     -Dlog4j2.formatMsgNoLookups=true
  4. Upgrade Log4j to 2.17.1 or higher
  5. Monitor /SMICM and application logs for exploit attempts
```

